WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.045 --> 00:00:03.412
- If you're afraid of coming
down and going and swimming

00:00:03.412 --> 00:00:05.616
in the ocean because of
sharks, you should be terrified

00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:07.811
to make a piece of toast.

00:00:07.811 --> 00:00:11.469
400 people died last year
from defective toasters,

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:14.552
whereas less than 10 did from sharks.

00:00:19.298 --> 00:00:22.014
This is the first large
shark expedition like this

00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:23.700
in the Western Gulf.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.346
We'll be tagging hammerhead
sharks, tiger sharks,

00:00:26.346 --> 00:00:28.963
and mako sharks, trying
to understand how they're

00:00:28.963 --> 00:00:32.031
leveraging the gulf and
their birthing, mating,

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:34.907
and full migratory patterns.

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:37.167
We'll be getting up every
day just before dawn,

00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:38.979
the fishing team will be
going out, getting all of our

00:00:38.979 --> 00:00:42.189
lines rigged and ready to go,
and then we'll get the ship

00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:44.872
in position with the lift in the water.

00:00:44.872 --> 00:00:47.115
And we'll be looking to capture,
bring sharks back to the

00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:49.035
lift, lift them up out of the water, and

00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:52.628
once we do that, a whole team
of scientists from a half-

00:00:52.628 --> 00:00:55.937
dozen institutions will
circle around the shark.

00:00:55.937 --> 00:00:58.941
And we'll conduct 12 research
projects in about 15 minutes,

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:01.699
then release the shark and
open source the tracking

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:05.366
to the world so that
everyone can follow it.

00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:08.480
What we're finding is that
these massive sharks have

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.940
huge, huge movements across the ocean.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.480
We have one of these white sharks, Lydia,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.433
who's traveled over 35,000
miles in just two years.

00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:18.386
Very, very regular for
these sharks to be swimming

00:01:18.386 --> 00:01:21.046
1,000 miles a month, which
is why it's so important

00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:23.823
to figure out where they go.

00:01:23.823 --> 00:01:26.509
We're losing 200,000
sharks a day for shark fin soup—

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:28.092
100 million a year.

00:01:29.824 --> 00:01:32.368
You know, people need to
understand an ocean with no

00:01:32.368 --> 00:01:35.359
sharks means an ocean with
no fish; they are the lion,

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:38.392
the balance keeper for the
ocean, and if they are not there,

00:01:38.392 --> 00:01:40.988
the whole food chain collapses.

00:01:40.988 --> 00:01:43.678
This is a simple battle;
we've got to solve the puzzle

00:01:43.678 --> 00:01:46.067
of these sharks’ lives to
make sure they flourish and

00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:48.136
make a difference for the future.

00:01:48.136 --> 00:01:50.553
(soft music)

